@meta
id: 01-10
title: Drill — overwrite without shifting
chapter: 01
type: drill
skills: mode-awareness, esc, survival-motion, cmdline
par: 16
minutes: 5
reps: 8
vscode: select the three characters, type three more
open: routes.md

@brief
Eight times: change the `500` on the refunds row to `422`, leaving the
column exactly where it is. Get in with `R`, type three characters, get
out with `Esc`.

Watch `-- REPLACE --` appear and go. The failure you are drilling out is
staying in replace mode a beat too long.

@keys
| `R` | replace mode |
| `Esc` | out |
| `$` `h` | end of line, one left |

@hints
The refunds row is two lines down. `$` puts you on the final `0`, and
two `h` presses put you on the `5` — the first character you overwrite.
--
Three characters in, then `Esc` immediately — before you look at the
result, not after.

@solution
    j j $ h h R 4 2 2 <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Fourteen keystrokes for a change VS Code would want a double-click and a
retype for, and the alignment cannot slip because nothing moved.

The cursor-then-type shape is worth noticing now, because it is the shape
of most vim editing. You spend keys getting the cursor to the exact right
character, and then the edit itself is tiny. Chapters 4 to 7 are almost
entirely about the first half of that sentence.

@start routes.md
METHOD  PATH            STATUS
POST    /orders         201
POST    /refunds        500
GET     /customers      200

@expected routes.md
METHOD  PATH            STATUS
POST    /orders         201
POST    /refunds        422
GET     /customers      200
